Every option works the same on `<AgenticChat>`, `<ChatProvider>` and `createChatRuntime`. A chat with most of them set:

```tsx
import { AgenticChat, createRestAdapter, type Attachment } from '@neofloai/atoms/chat';

// getAccessToken, refreshSession, openWorkspacePicker, connectSource and uploadToStorage stand
// for your app's own functions; currentUser, invoiceOnScreen and router for its own state.

const adapter = createRestAdapter({
  baseUrl: 'https://api.example.com',
  headers: async () => ({ Authorization: `Bearer ${await getAccessToken()}` }),
  // A 401 calls this once with its error code; return true once the user can continue, to
  // retry the request. `unauthenticated` means sign in again; a product code can mean more.
  onUnauthorized: ({ code }) => (code === 'askneo.workspace_required' ? openWorkspacePicker() : refreshSession()),
});

export function Chat({ conversationId }: { conversationId?: string }) {
  return (
    <AgenticChat
      adapter={adapter}
      // The composer's "All workflows" picker. Groups are workspaces, items are workflows.
      scope={{
        groups: [
          {
            id: 'ws_finance',
            label: 'Finance',
            items: [
              { id: 'ip', label: 'Invoice Processing' },
              { id: 'col', label: 'Collections' },
            ],
          },
        ],
      }}
      // Context from the page the chat is embedded in, merged into every turn.
      workflowContext={() =>
        invoiceOnScreen
          ? [{ workflow_name: 'ip', workspace_id: ['ws_finance'], context: { invoice_id: invoiceOnScreen.id } }]
          : undefined
      }
      // The standard has no upload endpoint, so your app uploads and returns the Attachment.
      attachments={{
        accept: '.pdf,.xlsx,.csv,image/*',
        maxSize: 20 * 1024 * 1024,
        upload: async (file, { signal, onProgress }): Promise<Attachment> => ({
          url: await uploadToStorage(file, onProgress, signal),
          filename: file.name,
          content_type: file.type,
          size: file.size,
          meta: {},
        }),
      }}
      persistence={{ storage: 'local', scope: currentUser.idHash, persist: { history: true } }}
      // Keep the open chat in the URL.
      conversationId={conversationId}
      onConversationChange={(id) => router.push(id ? `/chat/${id}` : '/chat')}
      // Cards call actions.emit(...); route those into your app.
      onAction={(action) => {
        if (action.name === 'open_invoice') router.push(`/invoices/${(action.payload as { id: string }).id}`);
      }}
      // A connected system (an ERP, invoice processing…) needs reconnecting: hand your backend
      // the credential, return true, and the chat retries the message once.
      onConnectionRequired={({ sourceId }) => (sourceId ? connectSource(sourceId) : false)}
      onError={(error) => console.warn(error.type, error.message, error.requestId)}
      labels={{ composerPlaceholder: 'Ask Neo anything' }}
    />
  );
}
```

## Options

| Option | Default | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `adapter` | required | The backend connection. Use `createRestAdapter` or your own. |
| `runtime` | | Pass a runtime you created yourself instead of `adapter`. See [Driving the chat from your app](/chat/configuration#driving-the-chat-from-your-app). |
| `blocks` | built-ins | Extra or replacement block definitions. Later entries win. |
| `scope` | none | Groups and items for the workflow picker. By default the selection becomes `workflow_context`. Change that with `scope.toRequest`. |
| `workflowContext` | none | Page context added to every turn. It merges with the scope by `workflow_name`. |
| `attachments` | off | `{ upload, accept, maxSize, maxFiles }`. This turns on the composer's + button, drag-and-drop and paste. |
| `persistence` | session storage | See [local persistence](/chat/persistence#local-persistence). |
| `conversationCreation` | `'eager'` | `'eager'` creates the chat when it opens, which is what fetches the greeting. `'lazy'` waits for the first message. |
| `hitl.composerWhilePaused` | `'disabled'` | Whether people can type while a card waits for a decision. |
| `conversationId` / `onConversationChange` | | A controlled active chat, for deep links. `null` means a new chat. |
| `onAction` | | Receives `actions.emit(name, payload)` from cards. |
| `capabilities` | from the config | Feature switches (`hitl`, `attachments`, `workflowContext`, `archive`, `cancel`, `perTurnModel`, `perTurnSources`). They decide what shows until the backend serves its config, then can only turn more off. |
| `onConnectionRequired` | | Called with `{ sourceId, code }` when a send fails with `connection_required` or `connection_expired`. Reconnect (for example `PUT /v1/connections/{sourceId}`), return `true`, and the message is retried once. |
| `onEvent` / `onError` / `onWarning` | | Telemetry hooks for every stream event, error and recoverable warning. |
| `labels` | English | Override any UI string, for copy changes or translation. |
| `navigation` | `'header'` | `'rail'` swaps the header bar for a rail on the right, with the history opening there. See [Header or rail](/chat/customizing#header-or-rail). |
| `railFooter` | | Your own buttons at the rail's foot. |
| `title` | the conversation's title | The title in the header bar. |
| `onTitleChange` | | Called with the conversation's title and id whenever either changes, so your app bar can show it. The id is `null` for a new chat that hasn't been sent yet. See [The title in your app bar](/chat/customizing#the-title-in-your-app-bar). |
| `turnActions` | all on | The row under each answer: `onFeedback`, `feedbackIssueTypes`, `copy`, `retry`, `timestamp`, `extra`, `visibility`, or `false`. See [Under each answer](/chat/customizing#under-each-answer). |
| `streamIdleTimeoutMs` | `60000` | How long a silent stream waits before it counts as dropped and becomes retryable. |
| `refreshOnFocus` | `true` | Refresh history when the window regains focus. |

## REST adapter options

`createRestAdapter` takes these options:

| Option | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `baseUrl` | The API origin. The `/v1/...` paths are appended to it. |
| `headers` | An object or an (async) function, called on every request so tokens stay fresh. |
| `onUnauthorized` | Called once on a 401 with `{ code, message, requestId }`. `unauthenticated` means sign in again; a product code (`askneo.gate_required`, `askneo.workspace_required`) says where else to send the user. Return `true` to retry that request. |
| `retry` | `{ retries, baseDelayMs, maxDelayMs }` for idempotent calls on 5xx, 429 and network errors. Message POSTs are never retried automatically. |
| `paths` | Override individual endpoint paths. |
| `credentials` | `'include'` sends the session cookie when the API is on another origin. |
| `fetch`, `timeoutMs` | Transport details. |

## Driving the chat from your app

Create the runtime outside React to reach it from anywhere:

```tsx
import { Button } from '@neofloai/atoms';
import { AgenticChat, createChatRuntime, createRestAdapter } from '@neofloai/atoms/chat';

// Create the runtime outside React to reach it from anywhere in the app.
export const chat = createChatRuntime({
  adapter: createRestAdapter({ baseUrl: 'https://api.example.com' }),
  persistence: { storage: 'session' },
});

export function AskAboutInvoice({ invoiceId }: { invoiceId: string }) {
  return (
    <Button
      onClick={async () => {
        await chat.newConversation();
        await chat.send(`Why is invoice ${invoiceId} on hold?`, {
          workflowContext: [{ workflow_name: 'ip', workspace_id: [], context: { invoice_id: invoiceId } }],
        });
      }}
    >
      Ask Neo about this invoice
    </Button>
  );
}

export function ChatPanel() {
  return <AgenticChat runtime={chat} />;
}

// On logout: stop streams and wipe everything the chat stored on this device.
export async function onLogout() {
  await chat.persistence.clear();
  chat.dispose();
}
```

The runtime also exposes `cancel`, `retry`, `reconnect`, `resume`, `selectConversation`, `renameConversation`, `deleteConversation`, `setModel`, `setDefaultSources`, `loadConfig`, the `chatConfig` store and `on('event' | 'error' | 'warning' | 'activeChange', listener)`. Several runtimes can share a page.

## A backend that doesn't follow the schema

`ChatAdapter` is the only seam. Keep the REST calls that match and translate the ones that don't:

```ts
import { createRestAdapter, type ChatAdapter, type StreamEvent } from '@neofloai/atoms/chat/core';

// legacyAsk(conversationId, question, signal) stands for your call to the old API.
// It resolves to { id, answer }.

const base = createRestAdapter({ baseUrl: 'https://legacy.example.com' });

// Keep the standard's calls that work and translate the ones that don't. The UI only ever sees
// the standard's events, so every block and hook keeps working.
export const legacyAdapter: ChatAdapter = {
  ...base,
  async *sendMessage(conversationId, body, { signal }): AsyncGenerator<StreamEvent> {
    if (body.message.type !== 'default') throw new Error('This backend has no HITL support.');
    const reply = await legacyAsk(conversationId, body.message.content.default, signal);
    yield { type: 'message_start', message_id: reply.id, conversation_id: conversationId, run_id: reply.id };
    const block = { id: `${reply.id}:0`, type: 'text', category: 'content', text: reply.answer };
    yield { type: 'content_block_start', index: 0, block };
    yield { type: 'content_block_stop', index: 0, block };
    yield { type: 'message_stop', message_id: reply.id, stop_reason: 'end_turn' };
  },
};
```

A block definition tells the chat how to render one kind of content block. Built-in blocks use the same API, so anything they do, yours can do.

## Example

```tsx
import { Button } from '@neofloai/atoms';
import { ReceiptIcon } from '@neofloai/atoms/icons';
import { AgenticChat, BlockShell, KeyValueList, defineBlock, type ChatAdapter } from '@neofloai/atoms/chat';

interface InvoiceCardData {
  id: string;
  vendor: string;
  total: string;
  status: string;
}

// Matches blocks whose `type` is "invoice_card". `parse` validates the payload; if it throws,
// the block falls back to the unknown-block renderer instead of crashing the thread.
export const invoiceCard = defineBlock<InvoiceCardData>({
  type: 'invoice_card',
  parse: (block) => ({
    id: String(block.invoice_id),
    vendor: String(block.vendor),
    total: String(block.total),
    status: String(block.status ?? 'open'),
  }),
  render: ({ data, actions }) => (
    <BlockShell
      icon={<ReceiptIcon />}
      title={`Invoice ${data.id}`}
      meta={data.status}
      footer={
        <Button size="sm" onClick={() => actions.emit('open_invoice', { id: data.id })}>
          Open invoice
        </Button>
      }
    >
      <KeyValueList
        items={[
          { key: 'vendor', value: data.vendor },
          { key: 'total', value: data.total },
        ]}
      />
    </BlockShell>
  ),
  toText: (data) => `Invoice ${data.id}: ${data.vendor}, ${data.total}`,
});

export function Chat({ adapter, openInvoice }: { adapter: ChatAdapter; openInvoice: (id: string) => void }) {
  // Define blocks at module scope, not inside render.
  return (
    <AgenticChat
      adapter={adapter}
      blocks={[invoiceCard]}
      onAction={(action) => {
        if (action.name === 'open_invoice') openInvoice((action.payload as { id: string }).id);
      }}
    />
  );
}
```

- **Matching.** Use `type` (one or several), `category`, or `match(block)` for anything else. Later registrations override built-ins, so `{ ...approvalBlock, type: 'approval_card' }` maps a backend's own name onto a built-in card.
- **Safety.** If `parse` throws, the block falls back to the unknown-block renderer. A renderer that throws is caught by its own error boundary, so the rest of the answer still shows.
- **Streaming.** `applyDelta(block, delta, snapshot)` takes over how deltas apply to your block type.
- **`actions`.** `send(text)` starts a follow-up turn, `resume(decision)` answers a checkpoint, `emit(name, payload)` reaches your `onAction`, and `retry()` retries the last failed turn.
- **The kit.** `BlockShell`, `KeyValueList`, `Shimmer`, `ChartCard` and `useChartPalette` are the same pieces the built-in blocks use, so custom cards look native. The last two come from `@neofloai/atoms/chat/charts`.

## Anatomy

```ts
defineBlock<TData>({
  type: 'invoice_card', // string or string[]; matched against block.type
  category: 'ui', // fallback when no definition claims the type
  match: (block) => boolean, // full control; checked before type and category
  parse: (block, chatBlock) => TData, // validate and normalize; throw to fall back
  applyDelta: (block, delta, snapshot) => block | undefined, // custom streaming
  render: Component, // receives BlockRenderProps<TData>
  toText: (data, chatBlock) => string, // used by Copy
  group: 'activity', // render consecutive blocks of this group together
});
```

`render` receives:

| Prop | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `data` | The output of `parse`, or the raw block without a parser |
| `block` | The runtime block: `id`, `type`, `status` (`streaming`, `complete`, `interrupted`), `checkpoint`, timings |
| `message` | The message it belongs to |
| `status`, `checkpoint` | Shortcuts for `block.status` and `block.checkpoint` |
| `isLast` | It's the last block of a message that's still streaming |
| `actions` | `send(text)`, `resume(decision)`, `emit(name, payload)`, `retry()` |

## Resolution

1. `match()` functions, newest registration first
2. An exact `type`
3. A `category`
4. The fallback (`unknownBlock`: JSON in development, nothing in production)

Because later registrations win, you can replace a built-in:

```tsx
import { AgenticChat, defineBlock, tableBlock } from '@neofloai/atoms/chat';

const myTable = defineBlock({ ...tableBlock, render: MyTable });
<AgenticChat adapter={adapter} blocks={[myTable]} />;
```

Or map a backend's own type name onto a built-in:

```ts
import { approvalBlock } from '@neofloai/atoms/chat';

const approvals = { ...approvalBlock, type: ['approval', 'approval_card'] };
```

## Looking native

Build cards from the kit so they match the built-in blocks:

- `BlockShell`: the card itself. It takes `icon`, `title`, `meta`, `actions`, `footer`, `collapsible` (with `defaultOpen={false}` to start folded, as the tool card does, or `open` and `onOpenChange` to control it), `tone` (`primary`, `warning`, `danger` for attention), and `flushBody` for edge-to-edge content.
- `KeyValueList`: muted keys against values.
- `Shimmer`: text still arriving.
- `ChartCard`, `ChartLegend`, `useChartPalette` from `@neofloai/atoms/chat/charts`.
- `Markdown`, `DataTable`, `ErrorNotice`.

Use Atoms components and tokens for everything else. `mode(theme, { color: text.default.b2 })` resolves an Atoms `{ light, dark }` token inside `sx`; `mode` comes from `@neofloai/atoms/chat` and `text` from `@neofloai/atoms/tokens`.

## Talking to the host app

`actions.emit(name, payload)` calls the `onAction` prop with `{ name, payload, block, message, conversationId }`. Use it for navigation, opening records, downloads, analytics: anything the card shouldn't own.

## Streaming your block

By default, a `snapshot` string replaces the block's `text`, a `snapshot` object is merged into the block, and `delta.text` is appended. Provide `applyDelta` when your block streams differently, for example a plan whose snapshot is a step array:

```ts
applyDelta: (block, _delta, snapshot) => (Array.isArray(snapshot) ? { ...block, steps: snapshot } : undefined),
```

Return `undefined` to fall back to the default.

## HITL cards

Inside `render`, `useHitl()` returns `canSubmit`, `isPending`, `isSubmitting`, `isResolved`, `decision`, `error`, `requires` and `submit(decision)`. `canSubmit` is true only when the card is the thread's live question and nothing is streaming. It also guards double submits. [Human-in-the-loop](/chat/hitl) has a full example.

To test a card against the mock backend, see [mock backend tests](/chat/mock#tests).

**Slots** replace any part of `<AgenticChat>`: `Welcome`, `UserTurn`, `AssistantTurn`, `Header`, `History` and `Composer`.

```tsx
import { Button, Stack, Typography } from '@neofloai/atoms';
import { AgenticChat, useChatActions, useThreadMeta, type ChatAdapter } from '@neofloai/atoms/chat';

function Welcome() {
  const meta = useThreadMeta();
  const { send } = useChatActions();
  return (
    <Stack spacing={2} sx={{ alignItems: 'center', py: 8 }}>
      <Typography variant="h4" component="h2">
        {meta.greeting ?? 'Good morning'}
      </Typography>
      <Stack direction="row" spacing={1}>
        {meta.suggestedPrompts.map((p) => (
          <Button key={p.label} size="sm" variant="secondary" appearance="outline" onClick={() => void send(p.prompt)}>
            {p.label}
          </Button>
        ))}
        <Button size="sm" onClick={() => void send('What changed since yesterday?')}>
          Daily digest
        </Button>
      </Stack>
    </Stack>
  );
}

export function Chat({ adapter }: { adapter: ChatAdapter }) {
  return <AgenticChat adapter={adapter} components={{ Welcome }} history={false} />;
}
```

- **Layout props.** `history`, `header`, `title`, `headerActions`, `maxWidth`, `composerProps` and `compactBelow`. Below 720px of container width, history becomes an overlay.
- **Compose it yourself.** `ChatThread`, `ChatComposer`, `ChatHistory`, `ChatHeader`, `WelcomeScreen`, `ChatSuggestions` and `RunStatus` all work inside a `ChatProvider`, in any layout you like.

## The empty chat

Until the first message, the built-in welcome holds the whole screen in one 640px column: the Neoflo mark, the server's greeting, the composer, and the suggested prompts under it.

- **The composer moves.** It starts under the greeting, three lines tall, and grows to 18 lines before it scrolls. When the first message goes out it docks under the thread, one line tall and growing to 8, and keeps the focus.
- **Each prompt is a row with its icon.** The backend picks the icon by name, from the list in the [backend contract](/chat/backend-contract#suggested-prompts). A prompt without one shows a sparkle.
- **The × removes a prompt.** It stays removed in every new chat on that device, as a preference (so `persistence` decides how long). Use `ChatSuggestions` with `removable={false}` in your own layout to keep every prompt.
- **Your own `Welcome` keeps the composer docked.** A replaced welcome is shown above a docked composer, as before, so it never renders a second one.

The placeholder is `labels.welcomePlaceholder` on the empty chat and `labels.composerPlaceholder` under a conversation; `composerProps.placeholder` sets both, as `composerProps.minRows` and `maxRows` set both heights.

## Under each answer

Each answer ends with a row: thumbs up and thumbs down, copy, ask again on the newest answer, and when it was given ("40 mins ago"). `turnActions` sets each part:

```tsx
<AgenticChat
  adapter={adapter}
  turnActions={{
    // The standard has no feedback endpoint, so thumbs up and down show only when you take the feedback.
    onFeedback: (f) => api.post('/feedback', f), // { rating, messageId, conversationId, runId, issueType?, details? }
    feedbackIssueTypes: [
      { id: 'wrong', label: 'Wrong numbers' },
      { id: 'slow', label: 'Too slow' },
    ],
    copy: true,
    retry: true, // or false, or (message) => yourOwnRetry(message)
    timestamp: true,
    extra: (message) => <TurnActionButton label="Share" onClick={() => share(message)}><ShareIcon /></TurnActionButton>,
    visibility: 'always', // or 'hover', which keeps older answers quiet until pointed at
  }}
/>
```

- **Thumbs up** sends at once; **thumbs down** opens a dialog for an optional issue type and details first. When `onFeedback` resolves the chat thanks the user, and it remembers the rating on the device.
- **Ask again** asks the answer's question again, as a new turn: the standard has no way to replace an answer. Pass a function to do your own thing.
- **`turnActions={false}`** hides the row.

## Header or rail

`navigation` decides how people reach the history and New chat:

- **`header`** (the default): a bar over the thread with the history toggle, the title and New chat. The history opens on the left.
- **`rail`**: no bar (your app shows the title). A narrow rail on the right has New chat, History and Search; the history opens on the right in its place, and Search opens it with the search field focused. `railFooter` puts your own buttons at the rail's foot, such as Help.

```tsx
<AgenticChat adapter={adapter} navigation="rail" railFooter={<HelpButton />} />
```

With the header, `title` replaces the conversation's title in the chat's own bar.

### The title in your app bar

With the rail, the chat's title goes in your app bar. `onTitleChange` gives you the conversation's title whenever it changes. Put it in a `NavbarTitle`, which shows whatever you pass, so your other pages can say "Home" or their own name in the same place. The id is `null` for a new chat that hasn't been sent yet, when the title is still "New chat"; show your own label then if you like.

```tsx
import { AccountMenu, Box, IconButton, Navbar, NavbarTitle, Stack } from '@neofloai/atoms';
import { SidebarSimpleIcon } from '@neofloai/atoms/icons';

const [title, setTitle] = useState('Ask Neo');

<Navbar>
  <Stack direction="row" sx={{ flex: 1, minWidth: 0, alignItems: 'center', gap: 2 }}>
    <IconButton variant="secondary" appearance="text" size="sm" {...sidebar.toggleProps}>
      <SidebarSimpleIcon />
    </IconButton>
    <NavbarTitle>{title}</NavbarTitle>
    <Box sx={{ flex: 1 }} />
    <AccountMenu user={user} onSignOut={signOut} />
  </Stack>
</Navbar>
<AgenticChat
  adapter={adapter}
  navigation="rail"
  onTitleChange={(chatTitle, conversationId) => setTitle(conversationId ? chatTitle : 'Ask Neo')}
/>
```

## Composer controls

The composer has the + button, two optional slots, and Send, which appears once there is something to send. Under a conversation they share one row with the input; on the empty chat they sit under it. Pass the slots to `ChatComposer`, or through `composerProps` on `<AgenticChat>`:

| Prop | Where it goes | Use it for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `menu` | The + button | See [The plus menu](#the-plus-menu) |
| `leadingActions` | After + on the empty chat; before Send under a conversation | A mode switch, a picker that should always show |
| `extraActions` | Right, before Send | A model picker, a mic |
| `scopePicker` | With `leadingActions` | Omit it for the built-in workflows (a submenu of +, or a pill with `menu: false`), pass your own node to put it in the row, or `false` to leave the workflows out |
| `footer` | Under the composer | A disclaimer, such as "Neo can make mistakes. Check important numbers against the source." A string is set small and centered. A function gets where the composer is, `'welcome'` or `'docked'`, so `(placement) => placement === 'docked' ? disclaimer : null` shows it only under a conversation. |

## The plus menu

+ opens a menu. Built in, it holds **Add files & photos** (when the chat takes attachments) and **Workflows**, the workflow picker in a panel with a search field. Pass `menu` a function to change it: it receives the built-in items and returns the menu, so you can add your own, reorder, disable or hide any of them. Items in different `group`s are ruled off from each other.

An item can open a panel beside it: a list of actions, or a picker over a value you own. Inside a panel, any row can hold `children`, which open in place under it, to any depth: folders and files, say. Give the children up front, or as a function that loads them the first time the folder opens.

```tsx
import { FolderSimplePlusIcon, NutIcon } from '@neofloai/atoms/icons';
import { AgenticChat, type ChatAdapter, type ComposerPickerItem } from '@neofloai/atoms/chat';
import { useState } from 'react';

// `api` stands for your own backend client.
const folders: ComposerPickerItem[] = [
  { id: 'bank', label: 'Bank statements', trailing: 5, children: () => api.listFolder('bank') },
  { id: 'close', label: 'September close', trailing: 2, children: () => api.listFolder('close') },
  { id: 'q3', label: 'Q3 target revenue.pdf' },
];

export function Chat({ adapter }: { adapter: ChatAdapter }) {
  const [files, setFiles] = useState<string[]>([]);
  const [tools, setTools] = useState(['web']);
  return (
    <AgenticChat
      adapter={adapter}
      // The chat doesn't send what is picked: send it the way your backend wants, here as context.
      workflowContext={() =>
        files.length ? [{ workflow_name: 'ip', workspace_id: ['ws_finance'], context: { files } }] : undefined
      }
      composerProps={{
        menu: (builtIns) => [
          ...builtIns, // attach, then workflows
          {
            id: 'folder',
            label: 'Folder',
            icon: <FolderSimplePlusIcon />,
            submenu: {
              searchable: true,
              searchPlaceholder: 'Search 17 files',
              picker: { items: folders, value: files, onChange: setFiles, cards: true },
            },
          },
          {
            id: 'tools',
            label: 'Tools',
            icon: <NutIcon />,
            submenu: {
              picker: {
                items: [
                  { id: 'web', label: 'Web search' },
                  { id: 'erp', label: 'ERP', disabled: true },
                ],
                value: tools,
                onChange: setTools,
              },
            },
          },
        ],
      }}
    />
  );
}
```

- **Folders open in place.** Clicking a folder's row, or →, opens it; ← closes it, or steps back to its folder. Its checkbox, or Space, ticks it.
- **A ticked folder stands for everything in it.** Its own id goes in `value` and its contents' don't, so files added to it later are covered. Unticking one file breaks the folder into the rest; ticking every file in a folder rolls them up into it. A folder with only some of its contents ticked shows a dash.
- **Search** finds rows in every folder that has been given or opened, and opens the way to them.
- **`cards: true`** shows what is picked as cards in the composer, beside any attached files, each with × to take it out. Leave it off for a setting like the workflows.
- **`maxDepth`** stops folders opening below a depth; a folder at the limit can still be ticked.
- **Hide or disable anything.** `hidden: true` leaves an item or row out; `disabled: true` shows it but stops it being chosen. Both work at every level, built-ins included: `builtIns.map((item) => (item.id === 'workflows' ? { ...item, disabled: true } : item))`.
- **No menu at all.** `menu: false` makes + attach files directly and puts the workflows back in the row as a pill. With every item hidden, + hides too.

Attached and dropped files show as the same cards: a tile for the file type, the name, and what it is (Spreadsheet, PDF document, Image, Document, Folder). `labels.fileKind` changes those words.

## Theming and copy

- **Theming.** Everything reads Atoms tokens, so it follows `NeofloThemeProvider`, including light and dark. Don't restyle the internals with CSS overrides. If Atoms lacks something, request it in Atoms.
- **Copy.** `labels` overrides any string. See `defaultLabels` for the full list.

When the backend emits a `checkpoint`, the card that asked the question becomes live. The turn stops with `stop_reason: "paused"`, the composer shows "Respond to the card above to continue", and the answer goes back through endpoint 6 (`POST /v1/conversations/{id}/messages`):

```json
{
  "stream": true,
  "message": { "role": "user", "type": "hitl", "content": { "checkpoint_id": "z93hdnf02c", "decision": "approve" } }
}
```

The rest of the turn streams into the same message. A double click can't send two decisions. If the resume request fails, the card shows the error and lets the user try again.

The built-in decision cards are `approval` and `choice`; their payloads are in [block contracts](/chat/block-contracts#approval). They need `hitl` in the backend's [config](/chat/backend-contract#3-the-config-endpoint-0): without it, the chat ignores `checkpoint` events and never shows approval or choice cards. The `hitl.composerWhilePaused` option (default `'disabled'`) decides whether people can type while a card waits for a decision.

## Your own decision card

Write your own decision card with `useHitl()`:

```tsx
import { Button, TextField } from '@neofloai/atoms';
import { PencilSimpleIcon } from '@neofloai/atoms/icons';
import { BlockShell, defineBlock, useHitl } from '@neofloai/atoms/chat';
import { useState } from 'react';

interface AmountData {
  title: string;
  suggested: number;
}

function AmountCard({ data }: { data: AmountData }) {
  const hitl = useHitl();
  const [amount, setAmount] = useState(String(data.suggested));
  return (
    <BlockShell
      icon={<PencilSimpleIcon />}
      title={data.title}
      tone={hitl.isPending ? 'primary' : 'default'}
      footer={
        hitl.isResolved ? null : (
          <Button
            size="sm"
            loading={hitl.isSubmitting}
            disabled={!hitl.canSubmit}
            // Sent as { stream, message: { type: 'hitl', content: { checkpoint_id, decision } } }
            onClick={() => void hitl.submit({ amount: Number(amount) })}
          >
            Confirm
          </Button>
        )
      }
    >
      <TextField label="Amount" value={amount} disabled={!hitl.canSubmit} onChange={(e) => setAmount(e.target.value)} />
    </BlockShell>
  );
}

export const amountCard = defineBlock<AmountData>({
  type: 'amount_confirmation',
  parse: (b) => ({ title: String(b.title ?? 'Confirm the amount'), suggested: Number(b.suggested ?? 0) }),
  render: ({ data }) => <AmountCard data={data} />,
});
```

Register it in `blocks` like any [custom block](/chat/custom-blocks).

Inside `render`, `useHitl()` returns `canSubmit`, `isPending`, `isSubmitting`, `isResolved`, `decision`, `error`, `requires` and `submit(decision)`. `canSubmit` is true only when the card is the thread's live question and nothing is streaming. It also guards double submits.

## After a reload

On reload, the history endpoint has to let the chat rebuild each card, including one whose checkpoint is still waiting for a decision. [Backend contract §6](/chat/backend-contract#6-history-and-hitl) lists what the chat reads to restore it.

`@neofloai/atoms/chat/mock` runs a backend on the standard in memory: the config endpoint, `selected_model`, `client_message_id` with duplicate detection, error envelopes and paired tool calls. Use it to build UI before the API exists, in tests, and in demos. The chat running on the [overview](/chat) uses it.

## Scenarios

```ts
import { createMockAdapter } from '@neofloai/atoms/chat/mock';

const adapter = createMockAdapter({
  latency: 200,
  scenarios: [
    {
      name: 'invoice',
      match: (prompt) => prompt.includes('invoice'),
      run: async (w) => {
        await w.status('Looking it up');
        await w.block({ type: 'invoice_card', category: 'ui', invoice_id: 'INV-1042', vendor: 'Acme', total: '$1,200' });
        await w.text('Here it is.');
      },
    },
  ],
});
```

The writer has `status`, `text`, `thinking`, `tool` (a `tool_use` and its `tool_result`), `block` (with streamed `snapshots`), `pause` (a HITL checkpoint with an `onResume` continuation), `error` and `wait`. Cancel and abort work the same as on a real backend.

## Config and connections

Pass `config` to change what the mock's config reports (`null` behaves like a backend without one). Its invoice-processing connection starts expired, so a prompt that mentions "invoice processing" fails with `connection_expired` until something calls `adapter.connect('invoice_processing')`, which is how the demo shows Reconnect.

